\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{|X|}
\hline
\textbf{Common Core State Standards:}
\begin{itemize}[label=$\bullet$, leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{HSF.LE.A.2: 2. Construct linear and exponential functions given a graph, a description of a relationship, or two input-output pairs (including reading these from a table).}
    \item \textbf{HSF.IF.C.8.B 8. b. Use the properties of exponents to interpret expressions for exponential functions. Apply to financial situations such as identifying appreciation and depreciation rate for the value of a house or car sometime after its initial purchase: $V_n = P(1+r)^n$}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Knowledge Needed:}
\begin{itemize}[label=$\bullet$, leftmargin=*, nosep]
    \item Properties of exponents
    \item Tier 2 vocabulary: growth/decay, appreciate/depreciate, increase/decrease, rate, interest, compound interest
\end{itemize}
\vspace{0.5em}

\textbf{Skills Needed:}
\begin{itemize}[label=$\bullet$, leftmargin=*, nosep]
    \item Facts about graphs -- x and y
    \item Use a calculator (number sense, operations)
    \item Converting \% to decimal and vice versa
\end{itemize}
\vspace{0.5em}

\textbf{Standards for Mathematical Practice}
\begin{itemize}[label=, leftmargin=*]
    \item 4. Model with mathematics
    \item 8. Look for and express regularity in repeated reasoning
\end{itemize} \\
\hline
\textbf{Unit Goals:} Exponential models are essential for interpreting real-world phenomena such as compound interest, population growth, disease spread, and depreciation. Without this understanding, students misinterpret trends and rely on linear relationships to make poor predictions in financial and scientific contexts. \\
\hline
\end{tabularx}

\vspace{-1.2pt}
